# REPAIR BRIEF r1 — Book 8, Movement 6 "Before the Paths" (ch34–40)

Same author, same method (NO-SOURCE: do not open `books/` or `v3-runs/`). You may read this edition's own closed text and the plan. The four Sol reads are in this folder (`plan-audit.md`, `source-tracking.md`, `review-editorial.md`, `review-cold.md`); read them before you touch a line, and read `plan-audit-apply.md` for what the coordinator took and declined. The baseline is `pre-repair-r1/` (your draft plus the seventeen coordinator-applied fixes). Write `REPAIR-REPORT-r1.md` when done and refresh AUTHOR-REPORT's "New canon minted" and "State at movement end".

**Verdict on the draft:** the audit ratified the architecture, the order of every day and every reveal, and all thirteen of your flags. Nothing is to be re-staged. The carts' street, the board, the stair, the night tail, the dinner, the light, Oryn's bench, the leaf, the chronicle, the street and the fire stand as you built them. This is a bounded repair: your sixteen markers are now protected text and need their joins; five passages track the source's order of telling and need their chains broken; and two small orientation cues. Leaving markers wherever a speaker was unpinned was the right call — three of the sixteen were not who the staging guessed.

Do not grow the movement. It stands at about 36,100 against a 34,000 allocation, which is accepted; the joins will add a little and C1–C5 should take a little out.

## A. KEPT (ruled; not to change)

1. **The twelfth leans at made things, unbidden, and stands as you wrote it.** The audit asked for all of it to be made deliberate (its fixes 18–36: the chamber, the rope-walk, the bridge, the handcart, the dividers, the inn table, the inventory line, the leaf). That is DECLINED. The plan says in terms that "the twelfth leans at every made thing … He learns not to look", and that Cael "catches himself reading made things everywhere". What the plan holds back for Millrace is the turn INWARD, upon his own still place; you never make that turn, and Cael refuses it once, deliberately, in chapter 35. Keep *goes / sits / bears / nothing*, the look he did not mean at the rope-walk, the dividers, "It'd look at a spoon", and the inventory's "it looks before I've said that it may". Never on a person; never inward.
2. **The chronicle (ch38).** Karis reads the line out and then will not read into the hole; "Marked." / "Marked.", Karis then Cleon. The editorial read called this a contradiction of "marked, not read". It is not: the line is protected text that must stand on the page once, and the plan's phrase means that the hole is not interpreted. Change nothing there.
3. ***The Architect*** spoken once by Karis, with Cael hearing it for the first time, exactly where you put it (the audit's fix 17 is declined).
4. All thirteen flags: the weigh-beam chalked to Cleon's name; Vastin's transport and stall; *and* / *set*; Seln's slip opaque; the Kindling beyond the Line; who heard the leaf; the watcher through the blue gate; Cleon's extra day; the length.

## B. Already applied by the coordinator (keep the protected words exactly; write the joins)

`{{JOIN}}` marks a place where a beat of YOURS belongs: a gesture, a breath, a look, a line of narration, or (where noted) a sentence or two of speech in your own words. Thirteen of them are on the page. None may remain. Never restore a join from memory of any other text; you have not seen one. Attributions outside the quotation marks ("Cleon said", "he said", "said Seln") are the coordinator's and are yours to change or drop.

1. **ch35, the board — Cleon's speech.** Now: `"A record," Cleon said, "sits." {{JOIN}} "An instruction *leans*." {{JOIN}} "This is a stair."` Only those three pieces are fixed, in that order, and Karis's "The strata lean too." follows them as you have it. At each join Cleon says more, in your words and in this edition's terms for the board (signs, marks, rows — the word "bearer" is not this edition's and must not appear). The sense he has to carry: a record's marks were put down after a thing happened, and they are still; an instruction's marks are each set down for the thing that comes next, so that the eye is carried along them in an order. He is looking between the tally-book and the Stair's ninth sheet when he says the last piece. Keep it short; he is a man of few words who is, for once, talking while he looks.
2. **ch35, "there".** Now: `"There," Cleon said.` It is Cleon's word, said where you staged it, with his finger at the end of the fifth row. Your "It came at that word." stands. Tidy the paragraph before it so the word lands as the finger stops.
3. **ch35:** `"No," Cleon said, to himself. "No. I'll not say that one. Not down here. Not to you."` — your staging was right.
4. **ch35, the warning.** The three spoken pieces are fixed; two joins between them. He is at the third table and has set the cup down; he speaks to the board. At the second join he stops himself.
5. **ch37, the cups — the line is CAEL'S.** Now: `"Out here you count who's owed a seat," Cael said. "It's a different ledger."` You wrote the lines round it so that Vastin or Brom could own it. Re-join so that Cael says it to Vastin, across the table, about the two cups kept under saucers: the man who audited seats for a registry is being told what the count is for on this side of the Line. Brom's "That's so" may stay if it still answers something.
6. **ch38, the leaf.** Both halves are on the page in italics inside Karis's quotation marks, with one join in each. NOTHING OF THE LEAF IS MISSING at either join. In the first half the record's sentence runs straight on from "the rivers ran wrong" to "there came one who saw the shape of it"; the join is where Karis breaks her own reading — a breath, a word or two of hers, or the pencil moving — or you may close it with a comma if she reads straight through. In the second half the join is the scraped place: she reaches it and says, in a few plain words of her own or with her finger above it, that the name is gone (four or five letters' width; the first upright stroke; nothing after). You described the object before the reading; do not describe it again.
7. **ch38:** `"It's a title, not a name; the name's gone."` Only that clause is fixed. It follows your "It was the first time that Cael had heard it spoken"; give her a join into it if the pencil's two touches need one.
8. **ch38:** `"Older," she said. "Made. Us."` — Karis's, said.
9. **ch38, Vastin from the box:** `"The apparatus," he said. "The measure, the seat, the tier, the mark. He made it and he set it over the land. And the mark that is given at the kindling — "` Karis's next line breaks it off, as you have it.
10. **ch39, the street — both lines are SELN'S.** Now: `"Made," said Seln.` and, on its own line, `"For a kind."` Cael tells him, in the fewest words it can be told in, walking at the handcart's tail; Seln hears it as he hears a report, says the one word, and then the other, and then nothing. Re-join the lines before and after, which were written so that either of them could own either line.
11. **ch40, Seln at the fire.** Now: `{{JOIN}} "I'll keep it a while longer."` Only that sentence is fixed. Before it, in the same speech, Seln says in your words what he has decided and why. The sense: the thing he carries is a thing a registry would pay for; the registry is built on that leaf; he had wondered what he was keeping it from; now he knows what the office is *for*, and that is worth knowing. It is the nearest thing to a decision anybody has heard him make aloud. The case is on his chest, shut, as closed chapter 18 has it ("He had carried that case for twelve years, shut"); print no count of years here and do not name what is in it.
12. **ch40, Vastin:** three fixed pieces with two joins (`"I believed it was imperfect."` / `"Now you're telling me it was *aimed*."` / `"At a category. Which turned out to be a person. Who's sitting across the fire."`), then `"No. I read it, after. I did not sign it."` / `"I would have."` with one. Check "sitting across the fire" against where you have Cael: he is standing against the stable wall. Either seat him before Vastin speaks or let the line stand as a thing a man says into a fire without looking; do not alter the words.
13. **ch40, pole two.** Cael's sentence (one join before "I happened anyway."), Lira's `"Yeah."` / `"Now what."` (one join), Cael's answer (one join before "I want to stand in the middle of it."). These are the book's hinge; the joins should be nearly nothing.
14. **ch37, the light.** The comparison to the chalk ring and the dark plate is cut (the audit's fix 37, accepted): it joined the station's instrument to the Kindling apparatus, which this book must not do, and it lent the light an attention. What remains: "There was a weight on him. He knew it, and he would have given a good deal not to. It was the particular weight of an instrument turning toward him." Smooth the paragraph if it now reads short, without restoring any likeness to a plate, a ring, an Arbiter or a thing that attends.

