   601	All of this is mine and is flagged for ruling.
   602	
   603	- **The carts' street and the beam.** From the lane's end a broader way opens to the left between the last house and the top wall: a shelf cut into the bluff on its outer side, paved with grooved setts, wide enough for two drays; up-traffic keeps the wall, down-traffic the kerb; it curls in under an archway to **a banded pole let down between two posts**, with the weigh-beam beside it (a pan as broad as a cartwheel on three chains; a man in a leather cap with a ruled slate, eleven years at that beam). His rule: "What goes by the slot's the hatch's. What comes on wheels is mine." **The honest way:** Karis lays the card on his ledge and says that it is matter for the lower archive on its practitioner's card; he reads the card's head with his lips and chalks four weights against that name. No name is spoken; none of the four restricted names is written. He will not weigh a man ("There's no column"). Vastin had given Cael the rule beforehand in the yard ("a load that travels on a practitioner's card is entered to the head of the card… it has an article, which I do not give you") and says one sentence at the beam and no article. Karis tells Cleon that his name is on a slate ("It's been on worse").
   604	- **The handcart.** The stable's own, two wheels and a pair of shafts, lent by the woman at the half-door while the loft is theirs, "if it comes back swept". Brom goes between the shafts (leaning up; walking backward as the brake down). Vastin rides on the boxes, facing backward, and is got off the wheels at the dock's corner.
   605	- **Vastin from the 291st.** Let down the loft ladder on a box-rope, with a turn round the ladder-post under Brom's weight; he does not go up it again and sleeps in the end stall of the stable, on straw, at no charge. He goes below because the card written the evening before says *four*. He comes down the inner stair on his seat, Cael taking his heel at each step, and goes up it backward on his hands. On the 293rd he takes the whole stair alone, a quarter of an hour early, and is three steps from the iron at the bell. He uses the road's hand-signs (*seen*; *nothing?*) from his straw.
   606	- **The three things the clerk has not asked** (the packet gives the fact; the three are mine): the name, rank and office laid on his ledge on the 285th; the same matter arriving by the bottom gate on wheels with another man's chalk against it; a bearer's hand and a hand's leg changed overnight, with a tread out of its bed and two bolts found in his cut. He writes *The same* and *four*.
   607	- **What Cleon learns of the 290th:** that Karis cut her hand on a stair ("Which lays?" / "The other."), and no more.
   608	- **Rune from inside, as Cael has it.** The twelfth does not ask; it leans, and gives single words (*goes*, *sits*, *bears*, *nothing*). A made thing is looked at from where it stands on the ground toward where it is going, which is why the light belongs low and to the left. A run's lean "comes down flat" where it ends; Cleon can therefore mark where each run stops (**"red"** squares of tile between signs), which gives Karis the lengths of the words.
   609	- **The decipherment, 291st–293rd.** Fifteen and sixteen on the 291st from a five-sign word and a second (the sixteenth is the old word for twenty, a score); seventeen at the bell. Eighteen to twenty-one on the 292nd. **Her first word that is not a number is *and*.** **The six-sign word that she had covered is *set*** ("as a stone is set, or a stake"); she had wished for a name. The twenty-second and twenty-third are spelled twice each by the leaf itself on the 293rd. She shows Vastin's copied page to Cleon only after the reading.
   610	- **The telling (35.3).** Cael's own account of why Cleon may be told and Vastin still may not: the taking from Cleon would go on every hour at his elbow, and his own term was that he be told what a thing is once he has looked; the eleventh sits unused and cannot be told without the whole. Brom: "Tell him what's his. I'd not tell him ours." Seln, that night: "I should have said yes… I should have been a good deal longer about it."
   611	- **The cup of water.** Cleon takes down the cup and the jar, pours, and sets the cup on the bare corner of the third table an inch from Cael's hand, without a word. Cael drinks half.
   612	- **How Cleon looks away.** At the board his eyes come as far as Cael's sleeve and wrist and he turns his whole head to the oak; on the 292nd they stop at the edge of the lamp's brass ("There's tar on my lamp"); on the stair he passes with his face to the wall; at the fire he keeps them off the boy for an hour.
   613	- **The tail.** Out by the stable's muck-door and a barrow-wide lane. Seln's three rules (look where he will be in twenty strides; never be the nearest thing that moves; when he stops, you do not). The watch: two men in leather caps, a handcart, a lantern on a pole (one is telling the other about a goose). The hood drinks at the trough's pipe; walks the brokers' street; **stands eleven minutes inside the rope-walk** (Cael counts seven hundred between two tar barrels); walks the quay past six boats and the ferry's cable; goes a quarter of the ring road, where the watch nods to him; warms his hands at the keepers' brazier; shows something low in his palm; is let through **a small door in the foot of the grating**, and turns in it for a breath. The blue gate at night: the grating down, a brazier, two keepers (a pike; a board on a cord). A keeper to Cael: "First bell… And you'll want your card." The groove is "a hand wide". Seln's finding: "It was worth one night to learn that it is not worth two"; the man was showing how much time they have. No face; no name; nothing located beyond "he may go through the blue gate by night".
   614	- **The parcel.** The carrier with his hat cut away on one side lies the night of the 292nd at the inn on his way to the western landing with the bag; he knows Brom's back from that landing and breaks the bag "for a thumb that the old man knows" (Brom's thumb, in hub-grease, on his way-slip; a copper of Cael's own). The dividers: two brass legs a hand long, flat on the inner side, points of blued steel in a round of cork, a broad flat rivet, a turned stalk. Karis asks for the loan of them "when I have a hide in front of me again". Cael does not open them on any distance (his ruling of the 168th).
   615	- **The meal.** Brom's ruling ("A parcel's come the breadth of the country to a hayloft, and found it. That's eaten on"); four marks at the half-door; the lower room; a goose, a pie, a white loaf, baked apples, cider; seven cups poured, two kept under saucers (Karis, above with her letters; Oryn). **Purse 130.**
   616	- **Seln's slip.** A slip of paper folded once, with close writing on one face, drawn from his sleeve at the table's end; read once; burned in the candle; "Closed." **What was on it is not given, and nobody asks.**
   617	- **The light.** A thread of the crown's light has come in each night by a gap at the left of the blanket and lain on the loft's boards like a chalk line. On the night of the 292nd it thickens and thins for a count of one. Cael feels "the particular weight of an instrument turning toward him" and answers himself ("A needle swings to iron. Nobody says that the needle has seen the iron"; a draught; a lamp-trimmer; his own tired eyes). He does not cross three strides of boards and does not look again. Lira crosses, without lifting the corner, and draws the blanket to the frame.
   618	- **Oryn's fourth gate** (unnamed): a larger hold, stone to a man's head with split oak above, a bench for twelve. The hold's old woman, who keeps its days, rings the fourteen-year-olds on a threshing floor of beaten clay "at the first light after the moon's full", with a hazel stick. No office, plate or clerk. The girl (unnamed; a plait; a sheepskin too big) steps in; her eyes go to a place a hand's breadth from her cheek; she listens; her knees give a little; she tells her grandmother in her ear. Oryn sits forty yards off with her hands in her sleeves and lays a hand on nobody. "Whether it needed the ring, Oryn had never learned. Nobody at any of those gates had been willing to try it without." She has watched forty such. **Her decision:** the fifth gate has its day, the ford an hour, and she will not come late to the mill; and the reading she owes will go "as deep as she went", asked first. She is two days behind.
   619	- **The leaf as a thing.** In a bundle between two thin boards of beech. A single leaf of skin the colour of old horn, stiff, curled at one corner, the lamp showing through it; written with a point and rubbed dark; twenty-six lines on its first face, in the floors' letters; a list on its back, not gone into. A little below the middle a place four or five letters wide scraped pale and thin, with one upright stroke left at its left side. Cleon: "One hand. One sitting. He ruled it first… a fair copy… scraped since it was written. I can't tell you by whom." Its head gives a year in the count that the old town's books keep. **Beneath the lower board: a dozen or more leaves of paper, a year-book of the town in its old ink hand** (floods, a murrain, who was reeve), which Karis reads at sight and puts later than the skin ("It speaks of the walls"). The chronicle's line stands on its first leaf beside a worm-hole or damp-hole a thumb wide.
   620	- **Who heard it.** Karis, Cleon, Cael, Vastin. Lira and Brom were in the eastern gallery with the carried lamp, putting twelve tally-books back (Cleon would not have the tape touched while a book was out of its bay). Karis reads it once, asks that nobody say it back ("A thing that is said twice begins to be said differently"), and makes two copies; the fair one goes into Cael's coat.
   621	- **The page the right way up (the §13 lock).** At the head of the inner stair on the evening of the 293rd, by the lamp in Lira's hand, Cael sees the first line of Karis's copy lying the right way up: "There was no desk on the other side of this."
   622	- **Cleon's day over.** At the bell of the 293rd: "They lie tonight… I said a week. I'll be a day over it. There's the back of that skin to do." (To meet the next packet's 294th.)
   623	- **The yard fire.** The stable's iron fire-basket on three legs, by the stall door; Brom's flint; upturned oak buckets; eight bowls ("I'd laid one for a mule before now"). Cleon comes in by the yard, having asked a keeper which street; he sits with his back half to the flame and eats half a bowl. Asked by Lira whether it is true: "I read how a thing's made… it's as old as it says it is… Whether he's told the truth in it, I can't say." His undertaking, to which Brom says "Good.": "That skin stays where it's lain… they'll not learn it from me." Brom's price: whatever was built was built over the whole country and down to every child; "Nobody spends so on a thing that he thinks is small… A price isn't a verdict… I'd want to hear the other side." After Lira's "You would have." she empties the old man's cold bowl into the pot and fills it hot. Cael does not write what he said.
   624	- **Roles (all unnamed):** the beam-man; the two watchmen; the keepers of the blue gate by night; the carrier with the cut hat (from Book 7); the hold's old woman, the girl in her year and her small brother; a girl in a red cap and a boy who laughed (Vastin's memory of kindlings he signed).
