editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md-88-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md-89-It was the first thing that she had asked at that board, and Seln answered her as he would have answered any of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md-90-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:91:"The four who were enrolled at a house above Ostrand. Not you. And not me; I was never enrolled there. I was employed. What waits for me is older, and it is not kept in a drawer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md-92-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:93:Lira had put her hand into her coat. She brought out a square of stiff paper, folded once, and opened it on the board, where a seal showed at its foot in dull red.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md-94-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:95:"Iron, Rank One," she said. "I got it on a plate in Ostrand with half a hall watching. Served how? What does the paper in the drawer do to this one?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md-96-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md-97-"It holds it. Your name goes into his book, and he finds it on his list. Before the hour is out he asks you, very civilly, what you carry under that name; and he gives you a slip for it, and keeps it, pending. You are not struck off. You are held. You may not fight on a floor, or draw on a house's purse, or put your name to a contract, until a thing is settled which is not yours to settle." He looked at Karis. "Yours is the same, and it costs you more, since it is the one paper that any archive would honour. And he," with a small nod at Brom, "is Copper in a book, and has a finding unsigned beside it, and both would be frozen as they stand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md-98-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md-114-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md-115-He had known it since a table at the top of a library, and he made himself say the rest aloud, in daylight, with a healer listening who had never been told it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md-116-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:117:"I stood in one at fourteen. It took eleven seconds over me, and wrote a word, and I've been that word ever since. I know what sits beside the frame now, and what it's for. If I stand in another, I come out as a column that somebody's chosen for me, with everything else written off. Or it goes dark again, and that's a second paper with a seal on it." He looked at the upturned bowl. "That's what I'm afraid of. It isn't the walls."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md-118-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md-119-"That is the price," said Karis quietly. "I said that it was more than the rest of ours together."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md-120-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md-2-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md-3-The page lay where it had lain all night, on its square of oilcloth at the bare end of the deal, and the first grey light out of the one small window showed Cael that nobody had moved it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md-4-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:5:He had slept beside it, after a fashion, on the meal-sacks. It was one line out of an old register, copied by a man who had then walked three hundred miles to lay it on that table, and it sent whoever read it to a town. He had got up twice in the dark to see that it was there, which was foolish, since a sheet of desk paper does not walk; and each time he had stood over it, keeping both hands by him, and had gone back to the sacks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md-6-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md-7-Karis came in before the smith's first hammer, with her gloves on already.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md-8-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md-30-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md-31-Nobody answered him at once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md-32-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:33:Cael read the slip a second time, as he read anything, and it was the last five words that he read. Two roads left the western landing. One ran away to the south and west for three hundred miles, to a ford and a station and a house with a seal, and an old man had come down it on a stick. The other was the border road. It went up over a moor to the Line by the short way, and Cael knew what stood at the end of it, since a small pale woman had laid her palm on a strip of oilcloth and told him; and last night he had read it again, in a square black copy, under a title.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md-34-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md-35-"Two days west," said Lira, with her lips. She did not say the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md-36-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md-38-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md-39-"Ours," said Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md-40-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:41:She had said it small and clear, with the flat under her arm. Cael had known her since a library table in Ostrand, and in all that time he had never once heard her claim a job. She had put her hand up for fences and for roofs when hands were asked for, and had gone where the count sent her; she had never looked at a slip and wanted it. Pike's pen stopped over the horn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md-42-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md-43-"Ours," said Brom.
