editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md-58-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md-59-Brom had a sigil in his pocket that would have made a stone of him. He had a certificate, somewhere, that said *Copper*, with a finding pinned to it that nobody had signed. He was twenty feet from a clerk who wrote down whatever was brought to his ledge; and he took a dray of slate on his bare shoulder, as a carter's son might have, with nothing to help him but his legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md-60-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:61:Cael had priced that man a great many ways since a yard above Ostrand. He had priced him by what he could carry, and by what he would take on his own skin for somebody else, and by a figure that a board had given him and a house had declined to sign; and he saw now that every one of those sums had had the grey in it. He had never once asked what the man was worth with the Path shut. It appeared to be a dray of slate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md-62-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md-63-"Now," he said, through his teeth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md-64-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md-100-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md-101-"It's a registry's yard."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md-102-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:103:"So it is. My master keeps eleven on his doors, and there's not one of them will shift a keg until he's lit up to do it, in front of whoever's passing; and then it's a line in somebody's book, every time." The clean hands turned the tally over. "The house is the third on the brokers' street, with the slate above its door. There is a guard's post at the store we keep inside the second wall. It's a standing pass, sealed, by the month. It's a bed in the house, above the wall, and the house's coat. Four marks, and found."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md-104-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md-105-Nobody spoke. Lira had got up off the paving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md-106-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md-286-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md-287-He wanted it tried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md-288-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:289:He looked at that squarely, with his breath going long and the flame standing up. Two feet from his right hand there was a pair of sore red eyes that could see what held a thing up. They had found it in a cutting that nobody living had made, off a drawing, in the time that it takes to swallow. He had a place under his breastbone that five readings had come to and none had entered. He had been told, in so many words, that those eyes were for walls and seals and boards, and not for boys. He believed it. He wished, all the same, that the grey head would turn two feet to the left and look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md-290-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md-291-He did not say so, since there was nobody to say it to. The man took no more note of him than of the iron arm that his own lamp hung from, and Cael had asked to be exactly that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md-292-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-10-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-11-"I am in no immediate—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-12-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:13:"I'll say when it's my turn to be told." She did not go nearer. "Two days ago, at a board, I opened my mouth about that leg, and you answered a thing that I'd not yet put. You said that you offered it to nobody. So I said that I still hadn't asked, and I let it lie, since that's my rule and I keep it." She pushed her left sleeve a little higher. "This is the asking. There are teeth in that, and under the teeth a joint that's been walked three hundred miles and then stood on by eight of those. I want my hands on it. And I want two fingers on your wrist while I'm there, to learn what's left in you. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-14-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-15-He did not answer at once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-16-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:17:Cael watched him look for the form of it. A man who had spent his life granting leave, and refusing it, under his hand and a seal, was being asked a plain thing by a woman on a road who had no office to ask it from. He looked at her bare forearms. He looked, for an instant, at the five who stood about him, and it came to Cael that he was waiting to hear whether any of them would answer for him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-18-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-19-Nobody did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-20-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-144-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-145-He had set his bowl on the sill. He spoke to the fire, and it was plain that he had been arranging the thing since the hound was lifted off his boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-146-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:147:"I shall tell you what that Path is for, since you have seen what it is not for. It is a Path of the tribunal floor. It is taught for fields; and a field is ground where a thing has been declared, a hearing, or a square, or a gate with a crowd at it. Everybody on a field knows where the line is. They are there because they want what lies on the far side of it: a door, or a prisoner, or a paper under seal. I have stood on eleven such. Nobody on any of them ever went round me. There was nothing round me that they wanted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-148-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-149-"And today there was nothing behind you that they wanted," said Seln. It was the first thing that he had said. "Only yourself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-150-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:151:Cael had read of such floors in a library above Ostrand, in the lists of other people's hearings, and had never thought to ask what stood at the edge of them. It appeared that an officer had stood there, with a stick, for the whole of a working life; and he had been so sure of being the thing in the way that he had never had to learn what a wall was worth when nobody wished to pass it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-152-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-153-"Only myself," said Vastin. "I drew a line across a road and stood upon it. It did all that such a line can do, and it served no purpose whatever, until a boy went and stood behind the middle of it, where he could not lift a hand, and made himself worth the crossing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-154-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md-36-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md-37-"I know you had. I was going that way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md-38-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:39:The light went gold, and then it began to go. Cael felt Shadow stir under its seal, as it stirred at every dusk, and every other sound in him dropped as a yard goes quiet under snow. He told it no, and it lay down. He had been telling it so for longer than two years, and it had never once left off asking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md-40-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md-41-He took Hesk's book out of his coat, and then put it back. There was a thing that he wished to do before he wrote.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md-42-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md-92-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md-93-He said it to the moor. They sat a yard apart, with the post between them, facing the same way.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md-94-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:95:"I read it for the first time in the week before I came up your bluff, on a road, by the candles of four inns, and for the last time on the morning when I left a seal in a tray. Between those two mornings I read it oftener than I have read anything that I did not write myself. I should like you to hear what was in it, since you have only ever seen it upside down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md-96-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md-97-"I've seen most of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md-98-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md-118-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md-119-"I declined to read you a second time," said Vastin. "You know that. What you do not know is why."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md-120-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:121:"You told us. In a room at Ostrand, with five of us at a table." Cael kept his eyes on the moor. "You said there were three things you'd not do. That was the third. You said it was the only sentence you had left that they still needed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md-122-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md-123-"I did say so, and it was true. It was the reason, and you are not to think otherwise for anything that I say next. Five young people had laid a case on a table that hour, and shown me what the office I served did with honest readings; I would not lend it my name for one more. I have let that stand for the whole of the matter ever since. It is not the whole."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md-124-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md-142-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md-143-"You wrote *declined*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md-144-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:145:"I wrote *declined*, on a form which has no line for why; and I should have written it if there had been no such night. The reason that I gave you at Ostrand was the reason. I do not take it back." He shut his eyes. "But when the pen was down I found that I was glad, and it was not on your account that I was glad. I was relieved that I need never learn what the plate would say. I had kept my hand off a door for a sound reason, and I was thankful of it for a poor one. I have been thought the better of since, by six people on a moor, for the half that I let them see. That is what I have carried from the seat to this bank, and it is not in the flat."
