   191	"I'll tell her. She'll have a good deal to say about it."
   192	
   193	"I should think the less of her if she had not."
   194	
   195	"The second is what they came for. I have stood at the foot of eleven fields, and I know when a crowd wants a door and when it wants a man, and those six wanted neither. They had a light, and they kept it dark, and they looked past me. I stood uncovered, a yard from one of them, with a face that forty years of clerks have been obliged to know; and he did not once look at it." Something moved at the corner of the old mouth. "I find that I was a little affronted. They were looking at you."
   196	
   197	Cael did not answer.
   198	
   199	"I do not know what they hoped to see, and I do not ask. I told you on a bank that I had measured a person and could not read him, and it is as true tonight. I sat four feet from you for three days, with a plate, and I have walked a shoulder off you since a ferry. I know that six people shut a stair to watch you do something, and that you went up a step into a crossbow's eye and did nothing whatever." He drew what breath he had. "I know no more of what you are than the brass did. That is the third head, and it is the one that I wished to say."
   200	
   201	He opened his eyes, and turned them, for the first time, to Cael's face.
   202	
   203	"I put the wall in front of it anyway. I'd like that entered somewhere, since I'm not the one who keeps the ledger any more."
   204	
   205	"It's entered," Cael said.
   206	
   207	"Thank you."
   208	
   209	He had said it so on a bank, with a hand on a boy's arm; he had no hand to put there tonight.
   210	
   211	Cael sat on.
   212	
   213	It was entered. He had not lied to the man by the width of a hair, since it was written already in the only place where such a thing was ever written first; and there was more in the entry than the man had asked for, or could have guessed.
   214	
   215	He had taken something from him. He had not reached. It had come as nine of the ten had come, out of standing within a stride of a person who was using what he had in earnest. It had come while that person poured himself into a line on a step, to keep a bolt off a face that he could not read. Cael had stood there and been given it.
   216	
   217	He could tell him. He looked at that squarely, as he had looked at a worse thing on a healer's plank.
   218	
   219	He had told the healer within the minute, with her palm still on him. But she had known already what he was; she had found the ten with her own two hands, and it had been hers to be told.
   220	
   221	This man knew nothing of it. He had not asked on a bank what read a still place, and he had not asked tonight what six people came to see. To say *I have your wall in me* was to say how it had got there, and how it got there was the one thing that Cael did not own alone. Five others held it. They had kept it out of every book for longer than he could say, and they had kept it above all from the office that lay on that straw with its hands open.
   222	
   223	He believed that the old man would have done nothing with it. That was not the question, since it was not his to spend upon a belief.
   224	
   225	So it was a debt, and of a kind that he had not carried before. He had owed a healer a telling, and had paid it. He owed this one, and could not pay; and the man had thanked him.
   226	
   227	He got up, and went back to his pallet, and took Hesk's book out of his coat.
   228	
   229	Lira was not asleep. She had turned on her right side, with the slow arm uppermost, and her eyes were open in the lantern's last light.
   230	
   231	"It's in there, then," she said, hardly above a breath. "Whatever it is that you've had on your face since the stair."
   232	
   233	"It will be. Give me a quarter of an hour."
   234	
   235	"Does he know?"
   236	
   237	"No."
   238	
   239	She looked across the loft, at a long shape under a blanketed window.
   240	
   241	"That's a lot to owe a man who can't hold a cup," said Lira.
   242	
   243	---
   244	
   245	Seln came down off the ladder's head before the pencil was wet.
   246	
   247	"I have a finding," he said. "I have looked at it by a lantern, and I shall look again by day; but I do not think that it will alter."
   248	
   249	He had brought the stave up under his coat, and he laid it now on the boards in the round of the light, with the cut that a long knife had made in its middle.
   250	
   251	"It is a watchman's, shortened, and the ferrule has been filed where a maker's mark would stand. The bolts are the same: a plain shaft, and no fletcher's thread." He put his hands into his sleeves. "Six people came into a stair in the oldest town on the Line, with their faces tied up, and not one of them opened a Path. They did not lack for them. I know how the one on the landing stood."
   252	
   253	"Why not, then?" said Lira. "There were six. They'd have gone through Brom like a door."
   254	
   255	"Because a Path that is opened under that wall is entered by whoever sees it; and they took the same care that we did to have nothing of theirs entered. They wished to have something of ours." His eyes went to Cael, and away. "The bow was to make one of you open, and the light was to tell somebody below that it had been done. When the light could not be shown, they had no reason to stay, and they went. That is their limit: they cannot be written down in this town any more than we can."
   256	
   257	Cael had the pencil, and he wrote it as it was said, under the day.
   258	
   259	"And the box?" said Brom, from the dark. "One of them had his fist through my becket. He never looked at me."
   260	
   261	"They were told to bring away what was carried. I do not think that they were told what it would look like." The mild voice did not alter; but the left hand had come out of its sleeve, and it rested for a moment on a strap. "What they would have been glad to find was on a roof above their heads, for the whole of that minute. I had not thought to be grateful to a parapet."
   262	
   263	Nobody asked him what he meant. Five of them knew; and whether the sixth, against the gable, was awake to hear it, Cael could not have told.
   264	
   265	"There is one thing more," said Seln. "I came down by my own way, and I was at this arch before you. There was a man on the step of the painted door, under its lamp, beside the book."
   266	
   267	"The one in the doorway," said Cael. "With the boots."
   268	
   269	"They were the same boots. He was not in a doorway tonight. He stood in the light, where a person stands who wishes to be seen, with his hood up; and he waited there until the five of you had come in under the arch, and then he walked away along the ring road without hurrying."
   270	
   271	"So they know where we sleep," said Lira.
   272	
   273	"They have known it for four nights. Tonight they have told us that they know. It is a civility, in that trade, and it is meant to be thought about."
   274	
   275	Cael thought about it. He had six people in a loft, of whom one could not stand, and another had seven stitches in her hand; and there was not a second house under that wall which would take them without a name. There were three days of a week, and fifty-two sheets on three tables, and a small pale woman who would go down to them tomorrow if she had to be carried.
   276	
   277	"We don't go up that stair at dusk again," he said. "Whatever else we do. I'll say the rest in the morning, when I can see it."
   278	
   279	"That is sensible," said Seln; and he went back to the ladder's head, and sat, with his face to the yard.
   280	
   281	Cael wrote the count first, as an old man in Denvash had taught him.
   282	
   283	*Two hundred and ninetieth day; Treswick; the loft; a hundred and thirty-four. The cut, at dusk: six, hooded, two from the yard and four from the lane, with staves, one bow and one dark lantern, and no Path opened by any of them. Nobody dead, none taken, no faces. Karis: the left palm, on a step's edge; seven stitches, Brom's; no healer, since a healer here wants a name. Brom: both shoulders. Lira: the right shoulder, on a wall, and the arm did not take it. V.: a wall, on the eleventh day of thirty; the knee has gone again, and he cannot hold a bowl.*
   284	
   285	*Finding, S.'s: they cannot be written here either. They wanted one of us to open, and a light shown for it, and a box; they got a wall and a rock, and they left when the light failed.*
   286	
   287	*They know the loft, and they've said so.*
   288	
   289	*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
   290	
   291	He ruled a line under that, and sat with the pencil over the leaf for some time. Then he wrote the rest, and it came all at once, as the largest things did.
   292	
   293	*Eleventh. Iron Wall, from Vastin, in an alley, while he held a line for us with nothing behind him but the decision to. It's not Brom's — Brom gets harder, this holds. Since my Kindling I've learned to give ground and disappear. Now I've got the opposite in me, from the man who spent forty years being the wall they put in front of people like me. He held it for us instead. I don't have a word for what that is. I wrote it down anyway.*
   294	
   295	Under it he put two lines more, since they were owed.
   296	
   297	*Not told. I can't tell the taking without telling the rest, and the rest is six people's. He asked for the wall to be entered, and I said that it was.*
   298	
   299	He turned the book round, and held it out across the gap between two pallets. Lira read it with her chin down, and she read the last two lines twice. She did not say anything at all. She shut the book over her thumb, and gave it back, and lay looking at the slates.
   300	
   301	The lantern was nearly out. By its last brown light he could see Karis, on her back, with a strip of a big man's shirt lying white upon her chest; and she was not asleep, for her lips were moving still.
   302	
   303	She had fourteen letters, and three days left of a week that a man below had called his own. There was a plank of black oak propped against a table in the dark, two hundred yards under her head, and a bundle on a shelf above it that nobody had untied.
   304	
   305	Cael lay down. He put the book under the box's becket, where an awl had mended it.
   306	
   307	*This far?* said the eleventh, of the dark.
   308	
   309	*No*, he said. He had a lamp to hold in the morning, and he found that he knew already which way it would be wanted.

