editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7357:    27	"There's nothing between them," Karis said. "That's the part I want everyone to see before we go any further. It isn't a corrupt correction. A corrupt correction has *reasons* in it — a second opinion, a challenged reading, a note from somebody's uncle. There's nothing here. There's a true reading, and there's an order, and there is nine weeks of nothing." She turned the pair over and set it down and took the next. "This one's twelve years old. Coastal station." She read four lines of it and stopped and did not read the rest aloud, and put it down, and took the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7514:    15	The maintenance leaf's *advisory corrections function* lived, its own text said, in the evaluation sub-layer — the stratum of the Arbiter protocol below the reading, below the classification, the maintenance level a practitioner never perceived and an evaluator never touched. The corrections fired at Kindling: after the architecture was read and before the classification reached a record a human saw, the function rewrote inconvenient architecture into a lesser Path, so that the falsification never occurred in a document, never crossed a desk, never required a corrupt official — because by the time any official saw the classification, the classification was already the lie, clean, native, indistinguishable from a true reading to every instrument that ran on the same sub-layer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7520:    21	"That's why no living official knows," Karis said. "There's nothing to know. No one falsifies anything. The falsification happened before the record existed, in a layer no one reads, on a schedule no one runs. Every official in the chain handles a true document. The document is just true about a lie." She set the leaf down. "It's the most elegant corruption I have ever seen described, and I want it on the record that elegant is the correct word and that I hate using it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-006/recheck-r1.stdout.log:7691:    17	"He is the best instrument the Compact ever made. Best instruments do not break where you expect. I have watched three men I would have sworn were incorruptible sign the thing that damned them, at the last, because the institution was the only self they had. Not one of them thought of himself as breaking. Each of them thought of himself as *holding*." He let the sentence finish in the room before he added to it. "I cannot tell you which he is. I can tell you that the only way to learn is the one that cannot be taken back."
