editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-006/REPAIR-REPORT-r1.md:47:12. **40:121–147, Vastin.** First join, his own words: "I held that for the whole of my service, and was easy in it. Above Ostrand I was shown that the fault was meant. I called it corrupt; I put down a seal upon that word, and walked three hundred miles on it, and thought that I had come to the floor." Second: "He had gone hoarse; he lifted his eyes from the fire for the last of it, and not before." Before "I would have.": "The raw hands had tightened upon the ash; he could have left it there, and it would have been true." **"Sitting across the fire":** Cael now comes round the basket for the page when Seln holds it out and sits on the bucket nobody had used; he is seated when Vastin speaks, and he **gets up off the bucket** to say pole two, which makes the standing a thing he does. Vastin's "You stood up to say it… I sat for mine" in the last scene is now literal.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-006/recheck-prompt.md:37:  b. **Vastin's join at the fire, ch40:** between the protected pieces he now says "I held that for the whole of my service, and was easy in it. Above Ostrand I was shown that the fault was meant. I called it corrupt; I put down a seal upon that word, and walked three hundred miles on it, and thought that I had come to the floor." Check every clause against this edition's closed Books 6–7 and Book 8 ch1–33 (what he was shown and where; what he called it; the seal; the distance).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/state/movement-002/review-editorial-prompt.md:3804:His arc: the man who enforced a corrupt system for legitimate reasons, and what he does when he understands the system is far more corrupt than he knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:175:He walked through it. On the near shingle a flat boat lay with its bottom stove in, and two black posts leaned beside it. A board had been left face upward in the grass. The paint had gone out of its letters, but they had been cut before they were painted. *Foot. Horse. Cart, loaded.* At the head of it was the registry's seal.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:191:He was not an inspecting officer. He had left the seal of his office in the tray marked *done*, some months ago, on a morning of rain, with nothing written to go beside it. His clerk had come for that tray at the usual hour. Whether what he had done was a resignation, or only a man going down a stair, was a question for an office to settle, and he had not waited to hear it settled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:259:It was the first valuation that anybody had put on him since he was a junior in a district house. He turned it over that night, sitting against a cart's wheel with the flat under him so that the damp should not come at it. He had been worth a seal, and a signature that a seat needed. He had been worth nine thousand findings. A man with ink on his cuffs had looked at what was left when those were taken away and had named the sum honestly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:39:The light went gold, and then it began to go. Cael felt Shadow stir under its seal, as it stirred at every dusk, and every other sound in him dropped as a yard goes quiet under snow. He told it no, and it lay down. He had been telling it so for longer than two years, and it had never once left off asking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:95:"I read it for the first time in the week before I came up your bluff, on a road, by the candles of four inns, and for the last time on the morning when I left a seal in a tray. Between those two mornings I read it oftener than I have read anything that I did not write myself. I should like you to hear what was in it, since you have only ever seen it upside down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:25:Cael turned the four sentences over while the stick came across the floor, since he had never heard that man waste one. A person who arrived in this country with a seal came over the board's head: he sent a clerk to the plank, or a paper, and had himself posted. This one had been told three times to go to twelve feet of oak, and had gone, and had read it from the left-hand post as any carter's boy would have; he had been asked what he had done, and had answered for himself, and had been taken at his word. He had paid a copper and a half for a loaf that cost the meadow a copper. The board had done to him what it did to everybody. Seln had wanted to know, before he stood away from a door, whether the man had noticed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:85:"I shall say the short thing first, since everything after it leans upon it. I hold no office." He did not raise his voice, and it had begun to lose its hoarseness. "I left the seal of mine in a tray, some months ago, and came down a stair. I gave no notice of it, and I was given no leave; whether that is a resignation or a man walking out of his place, the office will have settled by now, and I did not stay to be told which. I have no rank that will open a door for you, and no seal that will shut one. Nobody sent me, and nobody has sanctioned a word of what I am going to say; you had better know that before you believe any of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:97:"There is a thing that ought to be said, and I am the one of us who was trained to say it. In my old trade there is a grade of approach which begins exactly so. A man arrives with nothing: he has no seal, and no escort, and he limps. He has been wronged by the house that he comes from, and has left it; and he brings a gift, which is true, since only a true gift is ever opened." His hands were in his sleeves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:119:"I didn't say proof. I said what it costs. I'll take a thing that costs that much a long way before I ask to see its seal."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:213:"Then you will understand me the more readily when I say that my trail stops there. I could not follow it. An archive on the Line keeps a book at its door, as a gate does, and every reader is written in it with the office that he comes from. My name stood in such books for forty years as the certifying office. If I had gone to that door with the seal, the request would have been minuted, and whatever shelf I had asked for would have been held by evening. If I went without it, I should be an old man of no standing, asking for the lowest shelf of a border archive, and I should not be let past the porter." The left hand opened a little, and shut. "I have never been able to go where that line points. I have only been able to carry it to somebody who might."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:249:Cael could guess how a sitting had ended for him, on every day of forty years: with a paper to be signed, or a seal to be set, and a clerk at the door to gather what was to go down a stair; and then with the next thing, drawn toward him across a desk. There had always been a next thing. Cael had been the next thing himself, once, on a bluff, for three days.

