editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:15:Cael had his own stave, which was no longer than his forearm and hand, with a rag bound round the grip. He stood four strides off. Wind was in his feet, as it was at the start of everything; it leaned him away to the right, with a burst ready behind the lean, and he told it no and felt his own two legs take the weight instead. The read asked next, civilly, for the ground under that stave. He shut it. No Paths meant the ten, whatever he called them, and he had written so. They were not much. He had bought them on this grass, a touch at a time, from a seven-foot pole.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:17:She came at him as a staff is meant to be used, with both ends.
