    59	The eyes that looked back at him were tired. They were nineteen years old, and they had heard what he had heard, and they had gone back to holding a lamp.
    60	
    61	"I'll be a flight behind," said the boy. "I'll not come nearer unless you call."
    62	
    63	It was a hundred and ten strides to the cross-way, on the stick and the uprights. He put the strap of the tarred case over his head, so that it hung at his back. Then he turned about at the foot of the first flight, and let himself down, and began.
    64	
    65	It was dark from the second tread. He had no need of light; he had learned those steps in three days as a blind man learns a room. He set his two hands on the stone behind him, and thrust with the sound leg, and came up by one. The bound leg went before him, stiff, like a thing that he had been asked to mind for somebody else.
    66	
    67	A man who goes up a stair backward looks at where he has been.
    68	
    69	There was nothing to see but the dark at the foot, and a little grey in it where a lamp burned a long way off down a gallery; but he faced it, tread after tread, and it grew smaller.
    70	
    71	He thought about the walls.
    72	
    73	He had been a wall. He did not say that with any pity for himself; it was the plainest account of Iron Wall, and of his office, and of what a seat had used him for. A crowd came to a gate, or a hearing drew too many, and he was sent for. He struck his stick upon the ground, and whatever stood on the far side of the line stayed there. He had done it eleven times for a seat, and twice since for no seat at all. He had believed, each of the eleven times, that the thing behind him was the law, and that it was imperfect, and that it was a great deal better than what would come through if he lifted his heel.
    74	
    75	He would have to go over the eleven, one at a time, when he had a table and a month. It was the kind of work that he was good at.
    76	
    77	Nine. The first landing. He rested with his hands open on his thighs.
    78	
    79	What he could not get past was smaller than the eleven, and it had no file.
    80	
    81	He had met the boy across a desk, with a plate between them, on a bluff. He had read him for three days, with every instrument that the seat owned, and had written that the instruments could not reach him. He had declined to read him again. He had told the boy why, on a bank, at dusk: the sound reason, and the poor one beside it.
    82	
    83	He had thought, through all of that, that he was dealing with an error. A plate had failed upon one child, as a scale fails upon a weight that is out of its range. It had been his whole understanding of the case, and he had built a resignation and a road upon it.
    84	
    85	He stopped there, on the dark stone, with one hand lifted to the next tread. He could feel that the thought had a second half; and the second half was the sentence again, come round by another door. He saw it for what it was. *Draft nothing. Just look.* He put the hand down upon the stone, which was cold and dry and laid without mortar, and looked at that instead until the thing had gone by.
    86	
    87	He went on up.
    88	
    89	There was a matter that he could settle, and he gave the second flight to it. They would sit in a yard tonight. The big young man had said so at noon: there would be a fire by the stall door, since one of them could not climb a ladder; and he knew who the one was. He would be at that fire. The leaf would be read there by those who had not heard it. And the boy would be across the flames from him, with all of it to carry.
    90	
    91	He owed that boy a sentence.
    92	
    93	He began, from forty years' habit, to set it in order. There would be three heads. The first would deal with what he had believed; the second with what he had been shown above Ostrand; the third —
    94	
    95	He caught it at the third head, and very nearly laughed, alone, on a stair, in the dark.
    96	
    97	He would not draft it. He would say what was so, when the time came, in whatever words were in his mouth; and if it came out badly, it would at least not have been composed. He thought that the girl with the long knife would ask him something afterward. She generally did. He thought that he knew what, and that he would have to answer it truly, and that the true answer was not one that a man would choose to give across a fire.
    98	
    99	He did not prepare that either.
   100	
   101	Eighteen. He rested.
   102	
   103	The bell went while he sat there: one small flat stroke, a long way down, like a spoon on a cup. He heard boxes taken up. He heard the boy's step come as far as the stair's foot, and stop, a flight and a half below, exactly where it had been promised.
   104	
   105	He had nine more. He had never yet got so high before the bell.
   106	
   107	He set his hands behind him, and thrust, and came up by one. It hurt a good deal. He found that he did not much mind; it was only a knee, and he had been told to the day how long it would take. A healer had been exact with him. He had come to value that above most things.
   108	
   109	At the twenty-fourth step he could go no farther without a rest, and he sat with the case against his shoulder and listened to four people climb.
   110	
   111	They did not hurry him. A brown light came up over the second landing, and a big man's shadow with it. Nobody said that he had done well. He was glad of that. A pair of hands that he knew took the foot of the tarred case, without a word, and bore it up for the last three.
   112	
   113	---
   114	
   115	"They lie tonight," the man had said, at the bell, of the fifty-two sheets on their three tables.
   116	
   117	He had stood by his desk to say it, with the card for the morning in his fingers, and his eyes on the floor between two boxes.
   118	
   119	"I said a week. I'll be a day over it. There's the back of that skin to do, and I'll not have it done against a clerk's bell." He pushed the card along the slope. "He'll write *the same*. He'll have to find something else to write the day after."
   120	
   121	Karis had risen from the second chair. She had two copies, and she folded one of them in three, and held it out across the desk's corner to Cael.
   122	
   123	"That is the fair one. It is to be read by whoever was not here, and I do not mind in what order." She kept her eyes on the paper. "I should like it to be you who carries it. I think that it would be wrong in anybody else's coat."
   124	
   125	He took it. It weighed nothing. He put it inside his coat, with an old man's book and a pair of brass legs, and it lay there against his ribs like a thing a good deal heavier than either.

