editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:47:Three nights ago, in a stair, six people had come to see what that boy was; and he had stood upon a step with his stick, on the eleventh day of thirty, and put a wall in front of him. He had not known then what stood behind it. He had said so, on a pallet, when he could not hold a bowl. He knew a little more this afternoon. He found that he would do it again, knowing; and that in a day which had taken the floor out from under forty years, it was the single act of his that he could look at without wishing to revise.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:241:"She gave me a look," he said. "I told her that there's seven of us, with the one that's owed. She asked what the eighth was for." He set the board down on an upturned bucket. "I said that I'd laid one for a mule before now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:91:Nobody asked him how long a while was; he had given a great many words, for that purse. Cael came round the basket for the paper; there was a bucket beside the girl's that nobody had used, and he sat down on it at last, since his legs had been asking for an hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:175:He thought of Hesk's book, in his coat, against his ribs, with a pair of brass legs shut in its pages. Ever since a plate lit he had taken whatever was too large for him and put it there, a line at a time, in the order of its coming. He had done so with a word on a plate, and with twelve fragments, and with a place that did not move. He knew exactly how this would go down, if he let it: *Claim. Evidence. Ruling.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:243:Cleon said, "Tomorrow the rest of the leaf. The list of parts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:277:*Two hundred and ninety-third day. Treswick; the yard, and then the loft; one in the stall. A hundred and thirty. A leaf read once, below, by Karis. A copy made. Read at a fire by the three who had not heard it. The reader came up, and ate half a bowl. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:73:There was a mule in the inn yard when they brought the handcart in under the arch, and a man with his hat cut away on one side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:79:"I've seen that go up a bank," he said. "A fortnight since, at the western landing, with a box on it; and a tall girl, and a little one with a roll of hide. You're off the one-armed man's cloth. The eleventh."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:111:*Bears*, said the twelfth, of the rivet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:135:It was Brom's ruling, and he made it at the half-door with the purse in his fist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:143:"A hundred and thirty," he said, to the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:153:He set a saucer over each of the two cups, to keep the dust out, and stood them at the table's end. One was a small pale woman's, who was above their heads with a lantern and twenty-one letters. The other had been carried under the lashing of his box, as an empty bowl, for eleven days; and the person whom it was kept for was a long way north along a hill-foot, with a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:211:He had not meant to make the inventory that night. He made one at the start of a thing; and he could not have said, climbing the ladder, what was starting. But he had a twelfth, which was not yet in any count. He had a pair of brass legs in his coat from a man who had taught him to write the count first. And there was a bundle on a shelf, two hundred yards over his head, which a pair of square pale fingers would untie in the morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:217:*Treswick; the loft over the stable; one in the stall. The two hundred and ninety-second day. Eight days inside. A hundred and thirty: four to a dinner, on Brom's ruling, and worth it. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:221:*Nineteen. Twelve confirmed fragments.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:225:*One anomaly, as before. The still place. Not the Quiet. Not a fragment. Not touched since the waypost, and not to be until a healer has been asked.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:305:She ate what was brought out, and lay down beside the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:331:It was a small movement, a little to the left, as a person turns toward a voice. Her eyes went with it. They went to a place in the air that was just beyond the corner of her sight, and when they came there the place had moved, and they followed it. Oryn watched a girl of fourteen look at something a hand's breadth from her own cheek that not one of thirty people could see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:369:Oryn got up, and went to see to the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:13:*This far?* said the eleventh, of the ladder's head, where a line might have been set that nobody could have climbed past.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:99:The eleventh had asked at that slot. *This far?* It had asked in the voice of a bar going home into two sockets; and it had been quite right, since that was exactly what the place was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:123:Everything that the crown burned or ate or wrote upon came up that road. They met a wagon of split logs in the first hundred yards, and a string of four mules under meal, and a cart with nothing in it but stoppered jars packed in straw. Things on wheels kept the wall, coming up, and things going down kept the kerb. Nobody had painted a line there either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:131:He had to put them somewhere. The wall was on his right hand, and above the wall was the crown; and he knew, without having once looked, where on that crown a pale thing stood. He had felt it go round him all that way, like a lamp carried round a room behind a person's back. So he looked left, and down, at a town laid out in three steps to a brown river. There was the bridge. It was small and dark and it stood on five piers; and a square woman had gone north from a fork with a mule, ten days ago, and would be at a mill before he was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:233:The eleventh had nothing to do in that gallery, and it did it all day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:15:*Two hundred and ninety-first day. Treswick; the loft; one in the stall. A hundred and thirty-four. By the carts' street, both ways, on wheels: a weight chalked at the bottom gate to the name on the card, and to nobody else. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:17:He ruled a line, and wrote the twelfth's lines under it, in their order, as he had got them by heart on a stair, and added the two that were his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:27:*Twelfth. Rune, from the reader, at his own table, while he showed Karis how a row goes. Told: Lira first; then him. He is the first outside the six who has it from my mouth how one came. Brom and Karis and Lira gave their word for it on the stair. S. and Oryn to be told. V. is not told, and I have now said to a stranger what I could not say to the man it's owed to. That's heavier than it was this morning. It stands.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:45:"You have a twelfth," he said. "And you have told a registered practitioner of the Compact, beneath its oldest registry, how you came by it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:219:*This far?* said the eleventh, of the shed's mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:343:*No*, he told the eleventh; and he turned his back on a brazier, and went down the street.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:375:*Night of the 291st. Followed the boots, with S., round the whole of the lower town. All of them asked; Shadow asked for three hours. One look that I didn't mean, with the twelfth, at the rope-walk: a grain in each eye. Not counted. Nothing opened.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:379:*S.: they have time. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:67:It was the twelfth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:139:"Twelfth. His. Lean."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:183:"Then that's three," said Lira. "You'll have the other two to face. One's on a roof, and one's got a mule." She shifted the ring to her other fingers and back again. "Go on. I'll hold his light at the top till he comes for it. Leave the box."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:185:He slid the beckets off. Brom shifted along, and laid one forearm across the lid of the third box, with a purse in it under five thumbs, as a man lays his arm along a gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:211:"There's a twelfth. It came an hour ago, at the fifth row, when your finger stopped. It's a piece of yours. I can see which way a run goes. I looked at that row, and at your board, and I've looked at nothing since."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:243:"Everything I read is *made*. Stone, ink, seal — somebody built it, so I can see how. If that fragment ever reads something for you that shouldn't be *made* and reads it anyway — "
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:297:*Rune-adjacent. Cleon. Partial. Rune family. A made thing's lean — the direction a maker put in it; which mark bears, which is borne, which is nothing; whether a run sits or goes. Structure, not meaning: it will tell me a thing is an instruction and will not tell me what it instructs. Made things only. Not a river. Not a man. Costs the eyes, after. No use in a fight. Engagement field: inscriptive — the second field that is not a fight, after clinical; given, not taken. Tier equivalent unknown. Deployment: none. Twelfth.*
7:Cael went back to the stable wall, and turned his face from her; he had been given that much by three people in a gallery, and he could give it to her.
33:Lira turned the folded page over upon her knee. She found the place through the paper with her thumb, and kept the thumb there; and only then did she lift her head, and look across the basket at the stable wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:43:It had gone into him like a blade laid flat. He had been ready for a harder thing and a softer one, and not for a true one, said plainly, by the one person who had known him when he had nothing in him but a word in square brackets. Every clerk who had ever looked at him over a desk had looked as at a thing that might go off. He had taken it so, and had built himself, a year at a time, into somebody who would not: who stood in corners, and kept twelve doors shut, and told a Path no at a tar barrel with a man five yards away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:101:"I read how a thing's made," said the voice like a hinge. "I'll tell you that, and no more, since it's all I have. It's one hand. He ruled it before he wrote it. He pressed like a mason and he didn't hurry, and it's as old as it says it is; I'd put my name to that at any counter." The square thumbs went round the bowl's rim. "Whether he's told the truth in it, I can't say. A man can write a lie in a fair hand. I've a hundred and ten strides of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:295:There was a bearing on a calfskin, in the second box, with four rings on it and a line that ran off the edge, and a set of marks in a ruled square which nobody could yet read. There was a bundle on a shelf with its tape untied, and the back of a leaf to be gone through in the morning; and after the morning there was a gate, and a bridge on five piers, and a road that went the way they had come and farther.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:33:He took the bundle in both hands; it was thicker than a loaf, and tied across both ways, and it left a clean square in the dust where it had lain. He set it upon the slope of his desk, under the good lamp, and stood looking at the knot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:59:"One hand," said the rough voice at last. "One sitting. He ruled it first, with the same point; you can see the lines under the letters. It's a fair copy, and he didn't hurry." The square finger came to rest in the air above the pale place. "That's later. I can tell you that it's been scraped since it was written. I can't tell you by whom."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:139:She put her hand into the breast of her coat, and brought out a square of oilcloth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:141:Cael knew it, having laid his own palm upon it once, on a rise, with a town in sight. She unfolded it on the desk's slope, beside the skin, and there was a sheet of plain desk paper, folded twice, in a square upright hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:173:He went over it as he would have gone over a cut on a moor, from the left. There was a desk of black oak, sloped, and a lamp on an iron arm, with the oil a finger down. On the shelf above stood a cup, which he had drunk from, and a jar; and a clean square in the dust. Two lengths of brown tape lay out to either side of a leaf of skin with twenty-six lines on it. There were two chairs, of which one had a cushion that had been carried from the other. A man stood with his eyes shut. A woman stood with a strip of a shirt round her left hand, and the hand was flat on the wood, and it had left off shaking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:25:Cael gave the rest of his ruling then, with his hair still wet from the pump. He had promised it for the morning, when he could see it; and he had lain awake a good part of the night finding how little there was to see. He had six people, and a hill with two gates in it, and a card that was good for one day; and there was a slot in a wall at the top of that hill, twenty-two steps long, which had been shut on them at both ends like a box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:29:"That leaves the carts' street," said Seln. He stood by the pump, with his hands in his sleeves. "I walked it once, under a keg of oil. It is half a mile, round the back of the crown, and there is not a yard of it that is ever empty by day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:49:It was a square of card with a buff edge, and it had been written the evening before, a hundred and ten strides under a registry, by a man who knew nothing of what had waited for them in a stair. It gave a door, and a purpose, and one bearer, who was Karis; and under her it gave a figure. The figure was four. It meant Cael and Lira and Brom and the old man, by count and by no name, and it would be told off against caps at two gates and against boots at a hatch. Seln was on no card, and never had been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:89:"He fell on your steps," said Karis. "Above. He cannot walk the hill."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:93:The keeper looked at a leg like a bolster, and at a slat of box-wood bound along it with strips of linen. He did not ask on what steps. A porter who was hurt in that town was no affair of a gate, so long as the figure came right; and the figure came right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:105:"One, and four. Your lame one is riding. I told you that those steps were older than he is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:121:It ran round the crown on the outer side, under the top wall, cut into the bluff like a shelf; and it was paved with great setts, grooved by iron, and wide enough for two drays to pass with a hand between their hubs. On the right the hill went up in pale stone, with the backs of tall buildings on it and not a window lower than a ladder could reach. On the left there was a kerb, and a drop, and all the roofs of the district.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:123:Everything that the crown burned or ate or wrote upon came up that road. They met a wagon of split logs in the first hundred yards, and a string of four mules under meal, and a cart with nothing in it but stoppered jars packed in straw. Things on wheels kept the wall, coming up, and things going down kept the kerb. Nobody had painted a line there either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:131:He had to put them somewhere. The wall was on his right hand, and above the wall was the crown; and he knew, without having once looked, where on that crown a pale thing stood. He had felt it go round him all that way, like a lamp carried round a room behind a person's back. So he looked left, and down, at a town laid out in three steps to a brown river. There was the bridge. It was small and dark and it stood on five piers; and a square woman had gone north from a fork with a mule, ten days ago, and would be at a mill before he was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:285:The square thumbs were going, one upon the other, slowly, in the good light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:297:"Bring that," he said, to the chair by the wall; and one square finger went toward the ninth of the Stair's twenty-nine. "Lay it by this. Foot to foot."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:13:He had gone through this once already, with another matter. For eleven days he had not looked at a light on a crown, and he had not done it by shutting his eyes. He had done it by having somewhere else to put them, and a reason, twice a day, until his neck no longer set at an alley. He did the same now. He looked at a raw hand on a tarred case, which nobody had made, and at the back of a girl's neck, and pushed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:27:The clerk wrote *the same*, and *four*. The old man went down twenty-seven steps on his seat, with his heel in Cael's palm, and said at the foot that he believed he had improved upon his time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:43:The man gave her his joins from the second bell. He went along the Ring's eleven with his finger, and the Stair's twenty-nine, and at every place where a run came down flat he said *red*, and she laid a square of tile. By noon each of the fifty-two sheets was parted, in broken tile, into pieces of the length of words.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:93:A leaf from an order-pad was folded round the legs. It was in a square upright hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:157:He had his elbows on the board, and his eyes had gone down to the wood, idly, as a person's do who is full. It was an old table. It had been mended. *Bears*, the thing said, of the two trestles in the middle; and of the leg at the far end, by the old man's stick, it said *nothing*, as flatly as it had said so of a red square of tile.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:211:He had not meant to make the inventory that night. He made one at the start of a thing; and he could not have said, climbing the ladder, what was starting. But he had a twelfth, which was not yet in any count. He had a pair of brass legs in his coat from a man who had taught him to write the count first. And there was a bundle on a shelf, two hundred yards over his head, which a pair of square pale fingers would untie in the morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:239:There was a blanket on the gable window, hung on two nails by a girl on their first night, and it had hung there since. It did not quite meet the frame on the left side. Each night a thread of the crown's light came in by that gap, and lay across the boards a yard from his knee, pale and thin and as straight as a chalk line. He had never looked at it as a light. It was a mark on a floor, as the bars in the lane were marks on the cobbles, and he stepped over it going to his pallet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:257:He looked at the three strides. The old man slept in a stall now. The pallet by the gable had been rolled, and there was nothing between his knee and the blanket but boards. He could cross them. He could lift one corner of a horse-blanket, and see for himself whether the thing on the crown burned as it had always burned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:259:He had written a sentence at a fork-stone, in this book, at a square woman's asking, and had read it back to her. It did not say *the steps* and *the square* because those were the only two ways of going near. It said them because she had seen what he did with a question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:265:*Claim: the light on the crown was not steady, for a count of one. Evidence: a pale line on the boards of a loft, through the edge of a blanket; one witness, with tired eyes. No cause found. Ruling: hold.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:269:*The promise stands. Not the steps. Not the square.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:321:She was thin, and tall for her age, with her hair in one plait and a sheepskin on her shoulders that was too big. She was frightened, and she was trying to walk as though she were not. Oryn had a square face that showed little, and she kept it so; but she had looked for that walk, and it was there, as it always was. Every one of them came across the clay like a person carrying a full pail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:43:The man made a sound in his chest. It was not the grunt that he gave for a thing well answered, and it was not the other, which had been nearly a laugh. He had heard a thing that he had not thought of; Cael could tell so much from the set of his shoulders, and no more. He did not take it up. He bent again to the sheet, and one square pale finger came out over the paper, an inch above her pencil, as it went above the lines of his own page in the mornings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:57:Nothing on the paper had stirred; the signs stood where her pencil had put them, square and small, each in its place. But the fifth row had a way to it now, as a field of barley has a way when the wind has gone over it and every stalk still bows a little toward where the wind went. The white sign at the head stood up straight, and all that followed it hung from it, and inclined, each toward the next. At the row's end the lean came down flat, like a foot arriving on a floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:73:It was said to the chair by the wall. The finger had gone back to the head of the sheet, and it was coming down the rows now, stopping above each place where a run ended. At every stop Karis laid a red square of broken tile below her pencil, between one sign and its neighbour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:137:Cael went up two steps, until his mouth was at the height of her ear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:141:He had said it under the scraping. The old man was nine steps above them, with his back to the climb, making noise enough for three; they used the voices that a road had taught them, all the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:231:The square hands had opened on the table's edge, and shut. Then the man walked to his desk, with one hand before him, by habit. He reached up to the shelf under the iron arm. He took down the cup, and the jar, and poured. He carried the cup back along the tables, and set it on the bare corner of the third, a long way from any paper, an inch from Cael's left hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:245:He stopped. The square finger had come off the reed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:277:There was a little brown light at the top of everything, three flights up, where a girl stood holding a lamp for a man who had not yet come for it. It showed him the first landing. It showed him, three steps above that, a pair of boots, and a leg thrust out as stiff as a bolster.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:301:He went over what was new in it, a step at a time, since there was nothing else to be done on those steps. It had a field that he had never met. Nine of his had come out of fights and said so; one said *clinical*, and that had been a healer's hands. Here was a thing that had come to him off a sheet of paper, across a table, from a man describing what he saw. The lines had found a word for that. They had found two more that he read three times over. Every other piece that he carried had been got by standing where somebody was hurt, or hurting. This one said that it had been handed to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:325:Cael had set his boot on the nineteenth tread, with the case, and the lamp was above him, eight steps up, in Lira's hand. It was a small brown flame in a smoked glass. He had knelt beside it for six days. It went into his eyes now like a handful of sand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:327:He shut them. That was worse; they burned behind the lids, and the water came out from under, hot, and ran down beside his nose. He opened them upon a smear. There was a blot of light, and a dark upright thing that might have been a girl, and where the steps should have been there was nothing that he could have set a foot to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:341:It was given a step at a time, in the level voice in which a clerk reads a column: the right foot, and the left, and that the tread was dry. For eight days Cael had gone two steps below that stick, so that there should be something under it if it fell. He had not thought to find the thing turned about. He bore up his end of the case, and put his boots where he was told, and was brought up out of the dark by a person who could not stand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:5:He had come down half a mile of carts' street with his lids shut and one hand on the tail-board of a handcart, pushing when he was told to push. In the lane a girl's shoulder had gone beside his own through four alleys, as it always went. It was the one evening in that town when he could not have looked at the crown if he had been paid for it; and he had found that funny, under a wall, with the water running off his chin, and had not said so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:117:He stood on the step, where a person stands who wishes to be seen, beside a book on a stand that he had not written in. His hood was up, and he wore a long coat of no colour. Cael looked at the boots, since he had been told of them four times: a horseman's, cut high, and at each heel a square of paler leather where a spur's rest had been taken off with a knife.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:135:It was a great deal harder than not looking at a crown. The crown did not move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:201:The tar yard was a square of cobbles above the river, with barrels stood on end in rows and a black kettle on a cold hearth. He went in among the barrels and found two that stood a little apart, and put himself between them with his back to the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:209:The lantern burned on its post at the head of the quay steps, and under it the river went by with a noise like cloth being drawn over a table. He could see the shed's mouth, a square of blacker dark, twelve yards off; and nothing came out of it, and there was no sound of boots on boards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:243:He counted ten. The hood went on round, as slowly as it had come, to the wall and the quay steps; and the man went down the steps.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:245:At the foot, for the length of two strides, the lantern was on him from above. Cael saw a pair of horseman's boots go through its light, with a square of paler leather at each heel. He saw gloved hands, clasped behind, and the back of a hood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:267:Then he turned his back on the river, and went up a flight of steps between two warehouses, toward the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:271:They followed by the steps. There was a lane above, and at its head the ring road, lying under the second wall like a ditch under a dyke; and there the man turned right, and went along it at his stroll, with the pale stone going up on his left hand higher than a house.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:65:It was dark from the second tread. He had no need of light; he had learned those steps in three days as a blind man learns a room. He set his two hands on the stone behind him, and thrust with the sound leg, and came up by one. The bound leg went before him, stiff, like a thing that he had been asked to mind for somebody else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:139:He took the foot of the case for the last three steps. Nobody spoke of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:269:"I've not been below the second wall after dark since my hair was another colour. I had to ask a keeper which street." The man had put up one square hand between his face and the basket, against the light. "I sat an hour under that skin, when you'd gone. I've sat under it ten years. I found tonight that I didn't care to do it by myself."

