books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-13.md:27:"A record," Cleon said, "sits. Every mark's where it is because a thing happened and somebody put the mark down after. You read it and the marks are *still*. They describe. Nothing in them wants anything." His hand went flat on the board, on his ten-year piece, and his eyes went to the unfocused place between the board and the sheet. "An instruction *leans*. Every mark's where it is because a thing is meant to happen next, and the mark's pointing at the next thing. You put your attention on it and it's not still. It has a direction. It's built so that the eye goes *along* it, bearer to bearer, in an order, and you can feel the order as you can feel which way a stair goes in the dark by where the treads are. That's what I see. Not words. The lean."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-13.md:33:"There," Cleon said, at the row's end. "Left to right. Six bearers, in order. It leans. The whole row goes somewhere. A record doesn't go anywhere; a record's a heap. This is a stair." He took his hand off the board. "That's the inside. That's the whole of the Path, and I've never once shown it to anyone, and I've shown it to you because in a day or two you'll need to have seen a thing lean, so that when I tell you, at the week's end, which way all of it leans you'll believe your own eyes and not mine."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md:113:It should have found nothing. That was the card: *made things only; not a river; not a man.* He was a man. The still place was in a man. A record of a thing that was not made would read as what a heap read as, which was nothing with a shape, and he lay and waited for the nothing, and it was the only time in the reading that he was not lying still, and he knew it, and could not stop it any more than he could stop his eye going along six marks in a row.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-13.md:91:"I'm going to pretend you didn't tell me that," he said, "for your sake, not mine. But I'll tell you one thing for free, since you can apparently take my whole trade by watching me work it for a minute." He set down the cup. "Everything I read is *made*. Stone, ink, seal — somebody built it, so I can see how. If that fragment ever reads something for you that shouldn't be *made* and reads it anyway — " He stopped. "Don't tell me. Don't tell anyone. Just remember I said it."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md:65:"Out here you count who's owed a seat," Cael said. "It's a different ledger."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md:27:"*In the years of the unmaking, when the un-bound walked in the land and no wall stood against them and no law held, and the cities of the east were emptied and the cities of the west were emptied and the rivers ran wrong* — that's the record's phrase; I've read it four times and it says what it says — *there came one who saw the shape of it, and set himself to the ordering. And he made the measure, and the seat, and the tier, and the mark that is given at the kindling, that each should know what he is and none should be what he is not. And he set the apparatus over all the land, and the land was ordered, and the un-bound came no more.*"
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-03.md:77:"It's a word I have never read in any stratum." She said it as a finding about herself, which was the only kind she would make about that word tonight. "Not in the confiscated record. Not under the Line. Not in anything I copied at three floors. It's a title, not a name — the record says *the*, and a record that says *the* is a record whose readers already knew who it meant. That's all the page says. That's all I'm going to say it says." She looked at Vastin. "Treswick's lower archive. You said Treswick on the landing."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md:41:"Older," she said. "Made. Us."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md:83:"The apparatus," he said. "The measure, the seat, the tier, the mark. He made it and he set it over the land. And the mark that is given at the kindling — "
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-16.md:9:He had told Seln on the street, because Seln had been above and had not heard the leaf, and a thing that is going to be said at a fire is a thing the man at the gate should have before the fire is lit, so that he can face the road while it is said. He had told him in the fewest words a thing could be told in, walking, at the handcart's tail, and Seln had listened as he listened to a report, and had said, at the end of it, "Made," and then, "For a kind," and then nothing, and had gone ahead to the yard and had the brazier lit before the cart was through the gate.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-16.md:47:Seln said one thing. He said it from the yard gate, with his back to the fire and his eyes on the ring road, in the voice for an inventory, and it was about the cache, and it was the nearest thing to a decision that Cael had ever heard him make aloud. "There's a thing on my back that a registry would pay for," he said, "and the registry's built on that leaf. I'd wondered, for four years, what I was keeping it from." He did not turn round. "Now I know what the office is *for*. That's worth knowing. I'll keep it a while longer." That was all. He had looked at a door at night and had decided nothing, and had heard a leaf read, and had decided nothing again, and had said so, which was a thing Seln did not do, and Cael entered it where he entered the things that were true about Seln, which was a short page and had just got longer.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-16.md:67:"I believed it was imperfect." He said it as a man reads a line from a file he wrote himself. "That's what I told a boy across a table in a borrowed room in Ostrand, in the autumn, and it was the truest thing I had, and it had taken me forty years to arrive at. Imperfect. A machine with a fault in it, that had filed a boy wrong." He was quiet. "In Ostrand I learned it was corrupt. Not a fault. A hand on the scale, at the top, deliberately. I resigned over that, or walked, and I have spent a winter thinking that was the bottom of it, because a man who finds out the thing he served was corrupt thinks he has found the bottom." He looked at the fire. "Now you're telling me it was *aimed*."

